RANSAC -- bincv::estimateAffine2D against cv::estimateAffine2D(RANSAC)
======================================================================
OpenCV 4.8.0-dev, cv::getNumThreads() = 1; binCV is single-threaded
threshold 3.0 px, confidence 0.99, iteration cap 2000, seeded and deterministic

 HEAP PROBE SELF-CHECK -- known answers it must recover
   sees malloc, and free returns the bytes        ok -- peak 100008 B, released 100008 B
   sees operator new                              ok -- peak 50008 B
   peak live is not the sum of allocations        ok -- peak 10008 B over 10 calls
   balanced work nets to zero                     ok -- net 0 B

================ 200 correspondences, 30% outliers ================
 planted inliers 140 | binCV 140 (11 iterations) | OpenCV 140

 PEAK SIMULTANEOUSLY-LIVE HEAP OF ONE CALL (allocator-level; sees cv::fastMalloc)
   binCV          0 B   plus 56 B of caller-owned scratch, which is known
                     before the call via ransacScratchBytes()
   OpenCV     27256 B   allocated internally; not visible from its signature
   -> 486.7x smaller, counting binCV's scratch against OpenCV's peak live
   ATTRIBUTED: 6688 B of OpenCV's peak is the SEARCH and 20568 B is the REFIT
   (its refineIters=0 arm, on the same counter). Most of the ratio above is
   the refinement, which binCV does in closed form and on the stack -- it is
   not a leaner RANSAC.
   binCV's own stack is measured by essential_stack_benchmark, not here:
   192 B on x86-64 against OpenCV's 11 440 B. A heap-only figure would
   flatter a library whose rule is that kernels do not allocate.

 ALLOCATOR TRAFFIC DURING ONE CALL -- A SEPARATE CLAIM FROM THE ABOVE
   binCV       0 calls   the caller's buffer is reused across frames
   OpenCV     64 calls   per call, so ~1280/second in a 20 Hz frame loop
   Peak live says how many bytes must exist at once; traffic says how
   often the allocator is entered. Neither substitutes for the other.

 variant                       us/call   spread   vs OpenCV
 binCV                            7.24    29.7%       6.14x
 cv::estimateAffine2D            44.45    25.8%       1.00x
 binCV, search only               5.59    20.9%       7.96x
 cv::, search only                6.86    45.1%       6.48x
 READ THE LAST TWO ROWS BEFORE QUOTING THE FIRST TWO. Both sides refit, so
 this is like for like -- but the two SEARCH loops are close, and nearly all
 of the difference is the refit: OpenCV iterates Levenberg-Marquardt where
 binCV solves the same least squares in closed form, the model being linear
 in its parameters. "binCV's RANSAC is faster" is not what this shows.
 Accuracy against a planted transform is asserted in tests/test_ransac.cpp.

